      "type": "msg",
      "body": "Two things worth knowing here, and one discrepancy in the file you should be aware of.\n\n## 1. Which timestep are you plotting?\n\n`item['ts']` has shape `(C, T, H, W)`. The cell that plots it does:\n\n```python\nunnormalized_ts = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(item['ts'][:,0,...])\n```\n\nThe `0` there selects index `0` along the **T** axis \u2014 i.e., the *first* frame in the input stack, not necessarily \"now.\"\n\nTo know what that frame's actual timestamp is:\n- The reference timestep for sample `idx` is `train_dataset.valid_indices[idx]` \u2014 this is the flare-matched Surya timestamp.\n- The offset (in minutes) of each frame in the T axis is `item['time_delta_input']` \u2014 an array of length `T`. Frame `t`'s absolute time = `valid_indices[idx] + pd.Timedelta(minutes=item['time_delta_input'][t])`.\n- Important: per `helio.py:_get_index_data` (`workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py:617-625`), all but the *last* slot are randomly sampled each call from `time_delta_input_minutes[:-1]`. Only the last slot is fixed (always offset `0`, the \"now\" frame). So index `0` is **not** guaranteed to be the same offset across calls \u2014 you have to read `item['time_delta_input'][0]` each time, not assume a fixed value. If you want the \"now\" frame instead, use `item['ts'][:, -1, ...]`.\n\n## 2. How to control what gets plotted\n\n- **Which sample/event**: change `idx` in `train_dataset.__getitem__(idx)` \u2014 this walks through different flare-matched timestamps (`train_dataset.valid_indices`).\n- **Which frame in the stack**: change the second index in `item['ts'][:, <t>, ...]` (0 = first/random-sampled offset, -1 = \"now\").\n- **Which channels**: controlled by `cfg.data.channels` in the config \u2014 this is both what's loaded into the C axis and what `channel_order` uses to pick colormaps in the plotting loop.\n- **Which offsets are candidates**: `cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes` controls the pool that gets randomly sampled for all but the last T slot. Since it's random, calling `__getitem__(idx)` twice for the same `idx` can return different actual timestamps for those slots \u2014 if you want reproducible plots, seed `random` before calling it.\n\n## One thing that looks off\n\nIn `flare_dataset.py:136-137`, the lines that set `sample[\"forecast\"]` and `sample[\"ds_index\"]` are commented out:\n\n```python\n#sample[\"forecast\"] = self.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx][\"normalized_intensity\"].astype(np.float32)\n#sample[\"ds_index\"] = self.df_valid_indices[\"ds_index\"].iloc[idx].isoformat()\n```\n\nEarlier cells in the notebook (e.g. cell 28's saved output `dict_keys(['forecast', 'ds_index'])`) were captured before this was commented out. As the file stands now, `__getitem__` won't return `forecast` or `ds_index` at all \u2014 so the normal way of confirming \"which timestep is this\" (`item['ds_index']`) is currently disabled. If that was intentional (e.g. you're mid-edit for your own project), fine \u2014 but if you want that field back for sanity-checking timestamps, those two lines need to be uncommented."
    },
