      "id": "574edabc-6ee1-4b40-b2a9-f7bd871f3dab",
      "body": "Quick clarification before I give you an edit \u2014 lines 136-137 in `flare_dataset.py` currently do this:\n\n```python\nsample[\"forecast\"] = self.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx][\"normalized_intensity\"].astype(np.float32)\nsample[\"ds_index\"] = self.df_valid_indices[\"ds_index\"].iloc[idx].isoformat()\n```\n\nThat's the label/timestamp attachment we just talked about restoring \u2014 it has nothing to do with image orientation. So repurposing those two lines for a flip would mean losing `forecast`/`ds_index` again. A couple things to sort out:\n\n1. **Do you want both?** (restore the label lines *and* add a flip, as two separate changes \u2014 not one replacing the other)\n2. **Where should the flip apply?**\n   - Just for viewing in the notebook's plotting cell (cell 32) \u2014 a one-line change to the `imshow` call, no `flare_dataset.py` edit needed at all.\n   - Baked into every sample the dataset returns (affects training, not just this plot) \u2014 done in `__getitem__`/`_get_index_data`, not lines 136-137.\n3. Worth knowing: the base class (`workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py:410,650-653`) already has a `random_vert_flip` constructor flag that flips `ts` (and `forecast`) along the H axis (`axis=-2`, top-bottom) \u2014 exactly the \"reflect across the x-axis\" you're describing. It's forwarded automatically via `**kwargs` since `flare_Dataset` passes everything through to `HelioNetCDFDataset`. The catch: it's a random 50/50 coin-flip per sample (meant as training augmentation), not an always-on flip.\n\nWhich of these matches what you want \u2014 reuse `random_vert_flip` (random, existing), or do you specifically want every sample deterministically flipped (which would need a small code change)?",
      "type": "msg"
    },
