      "body": "Good catch \u2014 cell 40 doesn't actually use the variables from cell 39. Here's what's wrong:\n\n1. **Cell 40 ignores cell 39 entirely.** It recomputes its own `unnormalized_ts = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data(item['ts'][:,0,...])` \u2014 same expression as `flipped_ts` in cell 39, just under a different name. `original_ts` is never referenced anywhere.\n2. **The widened GridSpec doesn't get filled.** You changed `gridspec.GridSpec(4, 4, ...)` to `gridspec.GridSpec(4, 8, ...)`, but the loop right below it is still `for i in range(4): for j in range(4)`, so it only ever touches columns 0-3. Columns 4-7 stay empty \u2014 that's the \"doesn't look correct\" part, the grid got wider but nothing was told to plot in the new half.\n3. **Cell 41 is empty** \u2014 it looks like this is where the second plot (the original, unflipped version) was meant to go but hasn't been written yet.\n\nWhat to do next, without touching what already works in cell 40:\n- Leave cell 40 as-is (it correctly plots the flipped version \u2014 just mentally rename `unnormalized_ts` to \"flipped\").\n- In cell 41, write a second copy of that same plotting loop (`GridSpec(4,4)`, same `if/else` per-channel logic), but source it from `original_ts` (already computed in cell 39) instead of `item['ts']` directly.\n\nThat gives you two separate 4x4 figures \u2014 flipped in cell 40, original in cell 41 \u2014 stacked one after another, which satisfies \"side by side\" without editing the working cell.",
